# Pro/Import library: roll a die with ANY number of sides.
#
# The next layer up from d6-lib.xml, a die fixed at six faces: this one takes the number of sides as a
# declared parameter (d, default 6), so one library file serves a d4, d6, d8, d10, d12, d20, d100...
# An importing model picks the die by overriding d:   Import("roll-die.sgm", d="20")
# See two-dice.sgm for a model that imports it twice, once as it stands (a d6) and once as a d20.
#
# How it works: Uniform draws from [0, d), Calculate adds 1 to give [1, d+1), and ToInteger (floor)
# turns that into the whole numbers 1 through d, each equally likely. The +1 is its own step because
# a declared parameter only substitutes where it is the WHOLE value ($d), never inside an
# expression like "$d + 1". The declared value is written as a quoted string.
#
# Left at its default (d = 6), this gives exactly the same rows as d6-lib.xml for the same seed.
# The same library exists as roll-die.xml and roll-die.json; all three give the same rows for the same seed.
dataset.root = Roll
dataset.columns = "roll"
define.declare d = "6"
Base = Uniform(0, $d)
Die = Calculate(Base, "y[0] = x[0] + 1")
Roll = ToInteger(Die)
